A||||9||JohnnyK||Headlights in the winter||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||10-30-2000||12:30 PM||||I'm in Toronto, and it is going to get pretty icy here during the winter. Now, I assume the lights will freeze up in the winter (ice between the actual covers and hood) and was wondering what is the best way to leave them up. Can I put them up now, and then disconnect the motors or something? I know I could rewire them, but I want something simple.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000001||william_thorniii||10-30-2000||12:36 PM||||Johnny,<P>Simple is as simple does :~)<P>Seriously though, when you look at your headlight motors, you should see two different connections.  After raising the lights by flipping the switch, simply disconnect the single wire connection (not the multi-wire connection that powers the light motor)... and voila! It takes about 10 seconds per side after the front-compartment lid is open.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||3
Z||000002||stsmithgt||10-30-2000||12:52 PM||||Isn't that the BLUE wire?  I had a problem with a headlight motor running even after the lights went down. When I put the lights up, I disconnected the wire (I think it has a female plug on it.  It's been a long time since I've done the job)so they would stay up!  I read that somewhere, but I don't remeber where.  Probably on this forum.  Each light should have one and I don't think it was hard to get to either. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000003||Tigger||10-30-2000||01:17 PM||||Yep, I believe it's blue.  Open the hood/er trunk and turn the lights on and disconnect that single wire at the plug.  Wrap the plug ends with black tape to keep road salt and moisture out.<P>With the lights up, now you'll want to carefully raise and lower your hood everytime make sure nothing binds with the headlight doors.<P>There is a way you can rewire the circuit so you can leave them up all the time to avoid wear on the motors, I'm sure someone has the link to the site.  This should work for what you want to do.<P>------------------<BR>T-T-F-N, Tah Tah For Now!<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tiggerblue.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||william_thorniii||10-30-2000||01:52 PM||||Tigger,<P>The instructions of wiring in a switch to kill the motors is on the Michigan Fiero Club website.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||3
Z||000005||JohnnyK||10-30-2000||03:59 PM||||THanks guys. So when I do that, and hit the lights, they will just go on? (None of this crazy motor run on..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||286SE'S||10-30-2000||05:02 PM||||My brother in Niagara Falls used to open his hood and push the headlight doors up with his hand to break the ice sealing them shut. The headlights would go up on their own after that. Seems like it worked ok for him, he never had problems with the motors.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fformula88||10-30-2000||05:16 PM||||In 10 years of Fiero ownership in Buffalo, my father nor I have ever had a problem with the headlights freezing or otherwise not raising due to ice buildup.  Of course, we would always clear the snow off of them and if necessary, break off large amounts of ice (rare).  However, they always came up and kept working.  So I am not sure it is necessary to go thru the trouble to rewire the headlights.<P>If you still would rather them stay up to be on the safe side, simply turn the lights on and unplug the motors.  They will stay up when you turn the lights off, and the lights will work normally.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/formula2m6/FIEROSIG1.JPG">  <BR>1988 Fiero Formula||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||10-30-2000||06:56 PM||||when properly working the lights will take allot of abuse. They should cut out if the doors are frozen. The older motors torques switch should prevent damage but don't count on it. The Gen2 motor wouldn't care. They stop as soon as they hit anything solid.<P>Even with the Gen2 motors I leave mine up in crappy weather. We get allot of slush and ice around here more than snow storms. I'm not so much worried about freezing shut as I am about not filling the light wells with all that crap.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Bob Englert||10-30-2000||08:36 PM||||I have added a headlight control switch to all of my Fieros.  This way I can leave the lights up when running in the store for a moment or if the weather was bad. <BR> <BR>The instructions are available at the NIFE web site at:  <A HREF="http://home.xnet.com/~nife/articles/tt-controlpopups.html" TARGET=_blank>http://home.xnet.com/~nife/articles/tt-controlpopups.html</A> <P>This is very easy to do.  I just used a small black flat toggle switch from Radio Shack.  I mounted the switch behind the dash just behind the headlight switch.<P>With this mod installed I flip the switch down and everything works like normal.  When I flip the control switch up, the lights raise when I turn them on but stay up when I turn the lights off.<P>Highly recommended, particularly for the 84-86 style lights that are harder and more expensive to rebuild.<P>Bob<BR>2-87 GT's<BR>87 Coupe<BR>85 SE (For Sale)<P>||||reg||14
